        "SIGNALMENT": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNALMENT",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Any dog suffering from thoracolumbar spinal cord injury.",
              "pdf_page": 1304,
              "printed_page": 1222,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SIGNS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SIGNS",
          "canonical_heading": "SIGNS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Thoracic limbs—rigidly extended; normal gait and postural reactions (because the lesion is caudal to the cervical intumescence).",
              "pdf_page": 1304,
              "printed_page": 1222,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pelvic limbs—depends on the severity and location of the lesion; usually upper motor neuron in type, but may be lower motor neuron.",
              "pdf_page": 1304,
              "printed_page": 1222,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "In severe, acute thoracolumbar myelopathies, spinal shock may be present in addition to the Schiff–Sherrington phenomenon: there is an initial flaccid paralysis caudal to the level of the lesion, with loss of myotatic and flexor reflexes. In dogs and cats, spinal shock is uncommon and usually resolves within an hour, with more typical signs of upper motor neuron disease subsequently developing caudal to the spinal cord lesion.",
              "pdf_page": 1304,
              "printed_page": 1222,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "CAUSES": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "canonical_heading": "CAUSES & RISK FACTORS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Trauma secondary to vehicular accident and intervertebral disc disease—most common.",
              "pdf_page": 1304,
              "printed_page": 1222,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Vascular myelopathies (e.g., fibrocartilaginous",
              "pdf_page": 1304,
              "printed_page": 1222,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Schiff–Sherrington phenomenon is not a embolism, coagulopathies, etc.). prognostic indicator; prognosis is determined by the severity of signs caudal to the spinal cord lesion.",
              "pdf_page": 1304,
              "printed_page": 1222,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
